Networks compiled into crate track by burn-onnx (build.rs), all under permissive licences whose texts are next to
this file. Checked against onnxruntime on crops of a real frame, two crops per batch against one run each:
src/tests.rs `vs_onnxruntime`, largest output differences 1.6e-6 (VitTrack), 2.2e-4 (YOLOX-nano, outputs up to 3.2),
8.8e-6 (YuNet, up to 8.4). Person re-identification uses no network (last section).

object_tracking_vittrack_2023sep.onnx
  What: VitTrack, single-object tracker (OSTrack-style one-stream ViT), template 1x3x128x128 + search 1x3x256x256 ->
        confidence 1x1x16x16, box size 1x2x16x16, box offset 1x2x16x16. Contributed to the OpenCV model zoo by Pengyu
        Liu (GSoC 2023); OpenCV's cv::TrackerVit (modules/video/src/tracking/tracker_vit.cpp) is the reference for
        pre- and post-processing (BGR, ImageNet mean / std, Hann window on the confidence).
  Source: https://github.com/opencv/opencv_zoo/tree/main/models/object_tracking_vittrack
          (downloaded from the zoo's mirror https://huggingface.co/opencv/object_tracking_vittrack/resolve/main/object_tracking_vittrack_2023sep.onnx)
  Licence: Apache-2.0 ("All files in this directory are licensed under Apache 2.0 License", the directory's README
           and LICENSE) -> LICENSE-vittrack
  Training data: not named by the zoo. The contributor's training config (https://github.com/lpylpy0514/VitTracker
           experiments/vit_dist/vit_48_h32_noKD.yaml) trains on LaSOT, GOT-10k (its vottrain split), COCO 2017 and
           TrackingNet; in opencv_zoo PR #245 (https://github.com/opencv/opencv_zoo/pull/245) the contributor runs
           that config's checkpoint (OstrackDist_ep0300.pth.tar) beside the zoo's ONNX on the same input: "Two models
           are almost same", with near-equal outputs. GOT-10k: "licensed under CC BY-NC-SA 4.0. You are free to use
           the dataset for research purpose. If you want to use it for commercial purpose, please contact us."
           (http://got-10k.aitestunion.com/downloads)
  sha256: 2990f0b7cd44d92afa48cd97db6de7be113fc1d9594fddb74e2725c10478e91d

yolox_nano.onnx
  What: YOLOX-nano (0.91 M parameters), COCO 80 classes, 1x3x416x416 BGR 0..255 (no normalisation, YOLOX >= 0.1.1rc0)
        -> 1x3549x85 (x, y, log w, log h in grid cells of strides 8 / 16 / 32, objectness, class probabilities).
        Trained on COCO 2017 by Megvii.
  Source: https://github.com/Megvii-BaseDetection/YOLOX/releases/download/0.1.1rc0/yolox_nano.onnx
  Licence: Apache-2.0, Copyright (c) 2021-2022 Megvii Inc. (repository LICENSE) -> LICENSE-yolox
  sha256: c789161ed43c8269fcd4e67c67eeeb4e80c622da2eb296a20bc6007bd18a0b7d
  Considered: yolox_tiny.onnx from the same release (sha256 427cc366d34e27ff7a03e2899b5e3671425c262ea2291f88bb942bc1cc70b0f7,
  19.3 MB): on v627 frame 300 it found 25 people against nano's 21, at ~60 ms against ~17 ms per 8 tiles; not worth
  5.5x the size.

face_detection_yunet_2023mar.onnx
  What: YuNet face detector (Wu et al., "YuNet: A Tiny Millisecond-level Face Detector", 2023), 53 k parameters,
        1x3x640x640 BGR 0..255 -> per stride 8 / 16 / 32: class and objectness scores (sigmoid), box (x, y offsets
        and log w, h in cells), five landmarks. OpenCV's FaceDetectorYN (modules/objdetect/src/face_detect.cpp) is the
        reference for the decoding.
  Source: https://github.com/opencv/opencv_zoo/tree/main/models/face_detection_yunet
          (downloaded from https://huggingface.co/opencv/face_detection_yunet/resolve/main/face_detection_yunet_2023mar.onnx)
  Licence: MIT ("All files in this directory are licensed under MIT License", Copyright (c) 2020 Shiqi Yu) ->
           LICENSE-yunet
  Training data: WIDER FACE (YuNet's training code https://github.com/ShiqiYu/libfacedetection.train: "YuNet face
           detection training on WIDER Face"), "Creative Common License (cc by-nc-nd)"
           (http://shuoyang1213.me/WIDERFACE/)
  sha256: 8f2383e4dd3cfbb4553ea8718107fc0423210dc964f9f4280604804ed2552fa4
  Considered for licence plates: LPD-YuNet from the same zoo (license_plate_detection_lpd_yunet_2023mar.onnx, Apache-2.0,
  1.03 M parameters, sha256 6d4978a7b6d25514d5e24811b82bfb511d166bdd8ca3b03aa63c1623d4d039c7), trained on Chinese
  plates. Its outputs matched onnxruntime (1.1e-5), but on two Wikimedia Commons photos placed in an equirect as
  views 15°, 30° and 60° wide it found the EU plate on a car only at 60° (score 0.38; the zoo's threshold is 0.8) and
  a board of three black plates only at 15° (0.32 to 0.59), and on the samples (no plate near) it scored columns and
  façades at 0.6 to 0.74. Not included.

No model: person re-identification (src/reid.rs)
  What: LOMO, the local maximal occurrence feature of Liao, Hu, Zhu, Li, "Person Re-identification by Local Maximal
        Occurrence Representation and Metric Learning", CVPR 2015 (https://arxiv.org/abs/1406.4216), written by us
        from the paper: joint HSV (6 levels) and two SILTP texture histograms over 10 x 10 windows of a 48 x 128 crop
        of the person's box, the largest count per row of windows, three scales; a brightness normalisation in place
        of the paper's Retinex; each pixel weighted by how much its colour is the box's and not the strips' beside it
        (a crop twice the box's width). The tracker's gallery weighs the dimensions by a Fisher ratio fitted to its
        own features of the subject against those of the people next to it (in place of the paper's XQDA, which would
        be trained on public re-ID sets), once it holds 12 of theirs; before that it compares by plain cosine against
        a threshold of its own (0.63 in place of 0.5). 15120 dimensions, cosine similarity.
  Licence: nothing to license: no weights, no training data, our code (patents not checked).
  Replaces: osnet_ain_x1_0_msmt17.onnx (OSNet-AIN x1.0, torchreid, weights MIT), removed 2026-10-25 because its
        training data, MSMT17, is released for research only. No learned replacement qualified: each person re-ID or
        image-embedding model looked at has training data that excludes commercial use or is undisclosed, e.g.
        ImageNet ("Researcher shall use the Database only for non-commercial research and educational purposes",
        https://image-net.org/accessagreement.php), which DINOv2 (weights Apache-2.0) takes in "as is"
        (ImageNet-22k, arXiv:2304.07193 Table 15), DINOv3 samples and ImageNet-initialised re-ID models start from;
        web crawls whose publishers advise against commercial or deployed use (OpenAI CLIP's model card; LAION-2B,
        DataComp-1B); undisclosed sets (SigLIP 2's WebLI); synthetic re-ID sets textured from DeepFashion (ClonedPerson,
        UnrealPerson) or web images of unknown copyright (RandPerson). The clean learned path left: OSNet-AIN trained
        from random weights on people we render from CC0 assets (MakeHuman, Poly Haven), loaded as before.
  Measured against OSNet-AIN on the samples (tests/footage.rs, 2026-10-25), OSNet first:
    sep (everyone in the clip, tracklets linked by IoU, same person 2 s apart and more against people seen on one
    frame, plain cosine): v627 ROC AUC 0.9975 / 0.9948, same-person pairs above the 1 % false-match threshold
    96.7 % / 91.9 %, the person ranked first among the others on its frame 96.2 % / 97.7 %; v606 0.9940 / 0.9789,
    94.7 % / 81.2 %, 99.2 % / 95.9 %.
    reid (the subject against a gallery of its first 4 s, 4 to 17 s later): the subject most alike on v627 37 / 30
    of 37 key frames, v606 66 / 65 of 66; the find rule (OSNet 0.65, LOMO 0.5 by the weights, margin 0.05) passes
    the subject on v627 37 / 37 of 37 and v606 40 / 64 of 66, the others 1 / 6 of 899 and 0 / 6 of 768 (LOMO on
    v606 with weights from its 8 features of others). With nobody seen next to the subject yet (LOMO by plain
    cosine, 0.63): the subject 4 of 37 and 52 of 66, the others 1 of 899 and 0 of 768; 0.5 there would pass 561 and
    308 of them, as weights from the first 1, 3, 8 features of others would 802, 254, 20 of v627's 899.
    subject with X5_BLANK (v627 none and 5 gaps, v606 none and 3; scored against a reference track): back on the
    subject after every gap with both, 98.3 to 100 % / 98.3 to 100 % of the frames after the gap on it, never on
    someone else (the rest without a box).
    Dark-clothed subjects at night (v627 picks at yaw 21 and 127, with and without a 2 s gap at frame 150): LOMO
    holds the yaw 21 subject with and without the gap; on the yaw 127 pick, once the subject is lost at frame 153,
    it takes someone else in dark clothes (after the gap at frame 248, without it at 153 to 168; by plain cosine,
    the gallery holding 8 and 10 features of others) where OSNet finds the subject again. Re-ID per call in the
    tracker 1.4 to 3.3 ms (CPU) against 7 to 13 ms (GPU), median.
